# What this is: the PowerShell release-integrity lab -- scripts/install.ps1, the Windows
# installer, driven for real by pwsh 7.4.6 on Linux against the same self-built release
# fixture that scripts/install-integrity-in-docker.sh offers to scripts/install.sh.
#
# Reproduce with:  scripts/install-integrity-powershell.sh
#                  (needs pwsh, python3 with `cryptography`, curl, sha256sum, ip and
#                  unshare; no docker, no Windows, and nothing downloaded. The script
#                  re-execs itself once inside `unshare -rn`, so the whole run is in a
#                  network namespace whose only route is loopback.)
#
# Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, pwsh 7.4.6 (/usr/local/bin/pwsh), python3-cryptography
# 3.4.8 (the distro build). Commit under test: 4aa337a7 plus the working-tree changes
# to scripts/install.ps1 and scripts/install.sh that this lab found.
#
# Why this exists: `check-powershell-syntax.py` can only say install.ps1 parses, and
# before this lab nothing had ever executed its download-and-verify path. Parsing is
# not installing -- see the two defects below.
#
# What this lab does NOT cover, stated up front rather than left to the reader:
#   - [RuntimeInformation]::OSArchitecture asset selection. The asset name is pinned to
#     chaos-win32-x64.exe and served by the fixture; which name a real Windows host
#     would ask for is not exercised.
#   - Executing the artifact. The fixture's "binary" is a POSIX shell script, so the
#     post-install version check is not run; the lab asserts byte-for-byte equality of
#     what landed at ~/.chaos/bin/chaos.exe instead.
#   - The registry PATH write. Every run passes -NoPath.
#
# --- how it went, because the interesting part is the four runs that failed ----------
#
# Run 1 died at preflight: `python3: unbound variable`. The interpreter was resolved
# after the namespace hop, where PATH is rebuilt; resolved at the top of the file now.
#
# Runs 2 and 3 got 100 x `curl: (7) Couldn't connect to server`. Two separate causes
# stacked on top of each other: the readiness probe asked for a /probe/ path the
# mirror deliberately 404s, so `-fsS` never succeeded and the run started anyway; and,
# underneath that, a fresh network namespace has loopback DOWN -- binding to
# 127.0.0.1 succeeds while lo is down, connecting is refused. The probe now asks a real
# scenario for a real 200, and `ip link set lo up` is itself an asserted check.
#
# Run 4: every scenario failed, each blaming the last candidate's DNS error. The
# fixture artifact was ~130 bytes and install.ps1 refuses anything under `-MinBytes
# 1MB` before hashing it. Padding the fixture past 1 MiB fixed the lab and exposed the
# real asymmetry: install.sh had no size floor at all and would hash a truncated body.
# install.sh now takes the same 1 MiB floor, and check 35 of the shell lab fails if the
# two installers' floors ever drift apart.
#
# Run 5: three failures, all in the lab rather than the installer. The installed file
# is $BinName (`bin/chaos.exe`), not the asset name -- the digests had in fact matched;
# the no_sig needle missed because pwsh wraps a thrown message with a `| ` gutter and
# emits ANSI codes, which the log flattener now strips; and the both-hatches case fell
# through to a spurious exit 1.
#
# Run 6 was 30/30. Run 7, the one recorded below, is byte-identical.
#
# --- is the lab capable of failing? -------------------------------------------------
#
# Mutation test: the `foreach ($why in ($reasons | Select-Object -Unique ...))` block
# in install.ps1's Download-GitHubFile was deleted and the lab re-run. Exit 1, with
# exactly three failures -- html_sums, empty_artifact and the 404-visibility check --
# and all 27 other checks still passing. The error the user then sees is
# "last error: Resource temporarily unavailable (mirror.ghproxy.com:443)", i.e. the
# public mirror, while the candidate that actually answered was the fixture with a 404.
# install.ps1 was restored from backup and re-verified (grep -c 'why:' == 1) before the
# run below.
#

